---
title: Healthcare Access for All
order: 1
icon: heart
---

A mother in labour delivers on a Pumwani corridor floor while nurses search for basic supplies. This happens every day in Nairobi's public hospitals. Kenyatta National Referral, Mbagathi, Pumwani — all are chronically overcrowded, critically understaffed, and routinely out of essential medicines. Patients wait hours, sometimes days, for emergency care. This is not the Nairobi we deserve.

We will build a fully equipped health centre in every ward — staffed by at least one medical officer, three nurses, a clinical officer, and a pharmacist — open 24 hours a day, seven days a week. No ward will be more than five kilometres from a functioning public health facility. We will deploy a county-wide electronic health supply chain system that tracks inventory in real time and auto-reorders before stock runs out. Every health centre will have reliable electricity, clean running water, and working diagnostic equipment.

Maternal and child health will anchor our county health agenda. We will expand mother-and-child clinics to every sub-county, deliver free maternity care with dignity, and deploy community health promoters to visit every household in their assigned zone. No mother will die giving life. No child will succumb to a preventable disease because a vaccine was out of stock or a clinic was too far.

The cost of this transformation is modest compared to the cost of doing nothing. We will ring-fence 30 percent of the county budget for health, eliminate ghost workers from the payroll, and sign a framework agreement with KEMSA for transparent, timely procurement. This is not a campaign promise — it is a moral obligation written into the county budget.
